r to keep W1 and W2 in case I can form a Chi. So perhaps not discard them yet.\n\nLooking at other tiles: T5, T6, T7 is a chow. If I can form another chow. For example, if I have T3, T4, T5. But I don't have T4. If I discard T5, maybe I can wait for T4 or T6. But T5 is part of the existing chow. \n\nWait, if I discard T5, then I have T6 and T7. If I can get a T4, then T4, T5, T6 would be a chow. But I don't have T4. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe discard B4. B4 is a single tile. If I discard B4, maybe someone else needs it, but it's not part of any chow in my hand. \n\nWhat about B8 and B9? B8, B9 could be part of a chow with B7, but I don't have B7. If I discard B8, maybe I can wait for B7, but again not sure.\n\nLooking at the hand, the most promising part is the T5, T6, T7 chow. If I can form another chow or pung. For example, if I can get T3, T4, T5. But no T4. Or T6, T7, T8. I have T7, but no T8. Wait, the game history shows that the next player played T8. So maybe T8 is already out? Wait, the game history of next player is Play T8. So T8 is already played. But in unshown tiles, T8 has 3 left. Hmm, maybe the T8 played by the next player is one of them. So there are still two more T8s. Not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a pair. For example, I have two T3s, two T6s, two B8s. If I can form a pair, but need to have four chows and a pair. But I only have one chow. Not helpful.\n\nAnother thought: the hand has a T5, T6, T7 chow. If I can form another chow in the same suit. For example, T3, T4, T5. But no T4. If I discard T3, maybe I can wait for T4. But I have another T3. \n\nAlternatively, discard T3 and keep T5, T6, T7. Then maybe wait for T4 or T8 to form another chow. But not sure.\n\nWhat if I discard B4? Then my hand becomes [W1, W2, W5, W8, T3 (2), T5, T6 (2), T7, B8 (2), B9]. Still 12 tiles. Maybe I can wait for a specific tile. \n\nAlternatively, discard W5 or W8. They are single tiles. If I discard W5, maybe someone else needs it for a